# --- s015 Book I d.2 q.3 (= Ord. I d.2 p.2 qq.1–4, nn. 191–429)
n("n309","s015-01","Tertia questio secunde distinctionis","la","source","= *Ord.* I d. 2 p. 2 qq. 1–4 (nn. 191–429). Scotus's four questions are posed in the same order (n. 191 *utrum possibile sit cum unitate essentiae divinae esse pluralitatem personarum*, etc.); Gorris's 'third question' counts the whole second part as one *quaestio*.","verified")
n("n310","s015-01","Deuteronomi. 33","la","textual","The text is Deut 32:39 (*Videte quod ego sim solus*). B reads correctly *deut. xxxij*; A's *33* is a misprint, kept in the text and corrected in the translation.","verified")
n("n311","s015-02","noticia abdita","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 2 p. 2 n. 233: *secundum quod quidam intelligunt Augustinum de notitia abdita, XIV De Trinitate*. Gorris names the *quidam* as Henry (*Quodl.* IX q. 15) and William of Ware (*In I Sent.* d. 3, q. *utrum anima semper se intelligat*). The Henry reference is not checked; the Ware question is plausible but unverified.","to-verify")
n("n312","s015-02","Arguunt alii per rationem boni","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 2 p. 2 n. 250 (*Arguunt alii per rationem boni, quia bonum est de se communicativum*), answered in n. 256. Bonaventure argues from the self-diffusive good in *In I Sent.* d. 2 a. un. q. 2. Gorris's *q. 2* agrees with that, but the article structure has not been checked against the Quaracchi edition.","certain")
n("n313","s015-02","Henricus est quodlibeto 6° q. 1","la","source","Henry of Ghent, *Quodl.* VI q. 1. This is one of the anonymous arguments for internal production that Scotus reports and rejects. Gorris's attribution has not been checked against the Leuven edition.","to-verify")
n("n314","s015-02","adequationem actuum essentialium et notionalium","la","source","The opinion opening Scotus's reply to the fourth question, *Ord.* I d. 2 p. 2 n. 270ff. (*de numero productionum*). That it is Henry's (*Summa* a. 54 and *Quodl.* VI q. 1) is Gorris's identification, and it agrees with the Vatican editors' apparatus as generally reported.","to-verify")
n("n315","s015-02","de duplici predicatione","la","source","Bonaventure, *In I Sent.* d. 5. Gorris gives *q. 1*; in Quaracchi, d. 5 a. 1 q. 1 does treat whether the essence generates and distinguishes kinds of predication. The phrase *de duplici praedicatione* was not found in the Commissio Scotistica text used here. It may belong to a passage of the *Ordinatio* that Gorris read differently, or to an addition.","uncertain")
n("n316","s015-03","qu⟨ia in⟩","la","textual","A stain covers the end of the line in A. Supplied from B (*q2 in pře sůt*).","verified")
n("n317","s015-03","per Aug. nihil est quod seipsum gignat","la","source","Augustine, *De Trin.* I 1, 1: *nulla omnino res est quae se ipsam gignat ut sit*.","verified")
n("n318","s015-04","Ad hoc dicit quidam","la","commentary","The distinction between the intellect and will as *operativae* (immanent acts) and as *productivae* (*dicere*, *spirare*) appears in Scotus, *Quodl.* q. 1 and *Ord.* I d. 27. Gorris's *in primo quodlibeto* most likely means *Quodl.* q. 1, which deals with essential and notional acts, rather than a 'first book' of quodlibets.","to-verify")
n("n319","s015-04","6° ethicorum","la","source","The contrast between *agere* (immanent) and *facere* (transient) is in *Eth. Nic.* VI 4, 1140a1–6. The statement that an immanent act leaves nothing in a patient is closer to *Metaph.* IX 8, 1050a30–b2.","certain")
n("n320","s015-05","tres articuli condemnati Parisius a domino Stephano","la","historical","Stephen Tempier's condemnation of 7 March 1277. The three propositions are, in the *Chartularium* numbering, art. 81 (*Quod, quia intelligentie non habent materiam, deus non posset facere plures eiusdem speciei*), art. 96 (*Quod deus non potest multiplicare individua sub una specie sine materia*) and art. 191 (*Quod forme non recipiunt divisionem, nisi per materiam. Error, nisi intelligatur de formis eductis de potentia materie*). Gorris's qualifier for the third reproduces the decree's own wording. Whether the articles were aimed at Thomas was disputed even in the Middle Ages. Bishop Stephen Bourret revoked the censure in so far as it touched Thomas in 1325.","certain")
n("n321","s015-05","Commentator 2° de anima commento 2° et 3°","la","source","Averroes, *In De anima* II comm. 2–3, and Avicenna, *Metaph.* II. Gorris cites them as authorities against Thomas's thesis. How they bear on individuation by matter is not explained, and the comment numbers are not verified.","uncertain")
n("n322","s015-05","pro 2°. di. 3","la","commentary","A cross-reference to Scotus *Ord.* II d. 3 (the principle of individuation), and probably also to Gorris's own treatment of that distinction in Book II.","certain")
n("n323","s015-07","Ad primam questionem respondet affirmatiue","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 2 p. 2 nn. 375ff. The argument from pure perfection (*perfectio simpliciter* … *melius ipsum quam non ipsum*) is Anselm's (*Monologion* 15) as Scotus uses it.","certain")
n("n324","s015-08","2. de anima","la","source","Aristotle, *De anima* III 2, 425b12ff. (whether sight perceives that it sees) and III 4, 429b9 (the intellect can think itself). A's *2.* should strictly be *3.*.","certain")
n("n325","s015-09","post. 8. paginas","la","commentary","Gorris's internal cross-reference to his own later comment on formal non-identity (*Ord.* I d. 2 p. 2 nn. 388–410, esp. n. 403: *non est identitas formalis*). It shows that he planned the book's layout, page count included. We will check whether the comment really falls eight pages on when we reach it.","to-verify")
n("n326","s015-09","quinque requiruntur","la","commentary","Gorris's five negative conditions for formal distinction summarise Scotus's definition of formal identity in *Ord.* I d. 8 p. 1 q. 4 n. 193 (*includere aliquid formaliter… in ratione sua quidditativa*), together with d. 2 p. 2 n. 403. The third condition (*in quodam confuso*) is less usual.","certain")
n("n327","s015-10","solitudine nature diuine","la","source","Answering the *contra* of Deut 32:39; compare Peter Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 24 c. 3, and Scotus's reply in *Ord.* I d. 2 p. 2 n. 421ff.","to-verify")
n("n328","s015-01","Deuteronomy 32","en","translation","A reads *33*. The translation follows B and the Vulgate.")
n("n329","s015-07","pure perfection","en","translation","*perfectio simpliciter*: a perfection that excludes all imperfection. The usual English is 'pure perfection'.")
n("n330","s015-05","Lord Stephen","en","historical","Étienne Tempier, bishop of Paris 1268–79.")
